WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.550
CardioScatter starts with a two-electrode ECG tag
that carries no battery.

2
00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:11.080
A smartphone-connected reader sends a continuous RF pump
at 219 megahertz.

3
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.680
The tag never rectifies that RF into DC.

4
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:22.240
Instead, the ECG voltage between the electrodes
shifts the frequency of a parametric response.

5
00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:25.400
Because that response is separated
from the much stronger pump,

6
00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:28.550
the reader can isolate it and reconstruct the ECG.

7
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.170
And here it is: the ECG, recovered and displayed live
on the smartphone.

8
00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:49.720
Now watch what happens when we lift one electrode:
the recovered waveform disappears.

9
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:57.300
Next, we use the same tag and recovery pipeline
with two dogs and one cat. No hardware changes.

10
00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:02.300
Here, the smartphone recovers and displays
the dog's ECG live.

11
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.100
A clinical Holter records the reference
at the same time.

12
00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:17.680
With the second dog, the SDR captures the RF response;
the inset shows the measured recovered ECG.

13
00:01:17.767 --> 00:01:22.767
The cat uses the same SDR setup;
the inset shows its measured, recovered ECG.

14
00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.980
Across all three species,
the on-body tag stays fully passive.

15
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:33.780
Cardiac voltage shapes the RF response directly;
the reader supplies the RF and recovers the waveform.
